From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 01:02:36 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] Speak Celio-Client's acked protocol, and keep serving
 earlier clients

Since b057315 Celio-Client acks and retries what it sends, and since
the commits after it expects the same of the server: deviceData is an
array of packets both ways, deviceCommand and the session events come
with an ack (and session events with a uuid to drop retries by), and
only the partner is told sessionClose when a player leaves a started
session. The server did none of this. Worse, an emit with an ack
reaches a listener as (payload, ack), which rxjs' fromEvent turns into
the array [payload, ack]: every status looked like a duplicate of the
last (uuid undefined) and no frame was relayed. The current client and
this server could not link at all.

Client.fromEventWithAck() keeps payload and ack apart and learns what
the client speaks: an ack seen makes it an acking client; device
traffic without one, an earlier client. The session acks what it
receives, duplicates included, and sends each client what it expects:
an acking client gets arrays, acked commands in order with its data,
and acked, retried session events; an earlier client gets single
packets and plain emits, as before. A client is only told apart once
it sends device traffic, so an earlier one waiting for a partner may
see partnerJoined/partnerLeft more than once; it handles them as
states, so that is harmless.

Tested with Celio-Client's own suite (tests/, the server started from
this checkout): 25 of 25 pass, against 13 before. And with gbagate's
test_celio.mjs, two adapters relayed through this server, with the
current client at both ends, the client from before b057315 at both
ends, and one of each: frames cross both ways in every pairing.

